  {
   "id": "CRRPUAA_BAAJ",
   "from": "NicheMapR",
   "author": "6cec8ea3702ece93",
   "date": "2022-02-07T03:59:13Z",
   "subject": "Re: Simulating cave microclimate",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>Hi Nicholas,</div><div><br></div><div>The microclimate model assumes the ~2m deep soil temperature is the running mean of the past year of 2m height air temperature records, as a boundary condition. That should be a very good approximation to a cave temperature. But exactly what the temperature and humidity is will depend on how air is flowing through it, presence of water bodies in the cave, etc. It&#39;s one of the rare situations where the standard bioclim mean annual temperature is a very good approximation for the temperature experienced by an organism!</div><div><br></div><div>All the best,<br>Mike<br></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
