   "subject": "Re: Simulating cave microclimate",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks Mike, that makes sense! One more question. With the endoR function from NicheMapR, is it able to simulate <b>temperature-dependent torpor </b>(or hibernation; i.e., pronounced reduction, but maintained, physiological states like metabolic rate and EWL; see attached image for example) for a small-bodied mammal? For example, I would like to simulate yearly changes in torpor duration and overall energy expenditure under different climate models. During the spring-summer season, the animal does its usual thing, then in autumn-winter, it enters torpor after reaching below a body temperature threshold (e.g., normal body temp reduces by 5°C). Is there a way to simulate this?<div><div><br></div><div><div>I had a look at your paper Kearney (2019) <i>How will snow alter exposure of organisms to cold under climate change</i>, which talks about hibernation cost. But in the methods, the simulation ran for &quot;a hibernating 20 g endotherm at 5 cm depth&quot; and &quot;Basal metabolic rate was adjusted from the expected normothermic value at 37°C&quot;. So it seems that the simulated endotherm was in a hibernating state all year?  </div></div></div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div>Nicholas</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, 7 Feb 2022 at 14:59, NicheMapR &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nich...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
