div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, 7 Feb 2022 at 14:59, NicheMapR &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">nich...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>Hi Nicholas,</div><div><br></div><div>The microclimate model assumes the ~2m deep soil temperature is the running mean of the past year of 2m height air temperature records, as a boundary condition. That should be a very good approximation to a cave temperature. But exactly what the temperature and humidity is will depend on how air is flowing through it, presence of water bodies in the cave, etc. It&#39;s one of the rare situations where the standard bioclim mean annual temperature is a very good approximation for the temperature experienced by an organism!</div><div><br></div><div>All the best,<br>Mike<br></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Monday, February 7, 2022 at 11:13:16 AM UTC+11 Nicholas wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi everyone,<div>Is there a function in NicheMapR to simulate a hypothetical cave microclimate for a given location?</div><div><br></div><div>For example, lat = -33.818, lon = 150.022</div><div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div>Nicholas<br clear=\"all\"><div><br></div>-- <br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><b><span style=\"font-size:10pt;background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\" color=\"#38761d\">Nicholas C. Wu</font></span></b><b style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:7.5pt;color:green;background-im